---
title: Sunday gravy with oxtail and sausage
tags: [italian, pork, weekend]
cuisine: italian
source: Paul's Sunday Gravy by Our Sunday Gravy, https://www.oursundaygravy.com/post/paul-s-sunday-gravy
---

Heat the oven to 350°F. Dry the @oxtail{1/2%lb} and season it with @salt{} and
@black pepper{}. Crush the @San Marzano tomatoes{56%oz}(two 28 oz cans) by
hand in a bowl.

Heat the @olive oil{3%tbsp} in a #Dutch oven{} over medium-high heat until it
shimmers. Brown the oxtail and @Italian sausage{6%links}(3 hot, 3 sweet) in
batches, uncovered, turning each piece once it releases, ~{5-7%minutes} a
side. Set the meat aside.

Cook the @onion{1}(large, diced) in the fat ~{5-7%minutes}, stirring, until
soft.

Add the @anchovy fillets{1}(with a little of its oil),
@red pepper flakes{1%tsp} and @oregano{1%tsp}, and cook ~{1%minute}.
Add the @garlic{4%cloves}(large, minced) and stir until fragrant. Stir in the
@tomato paste{1/2%oz} for ~{1%minute}; the pot should be well browned.

Raise the heat and deglaze with the @red wine{1/2%cup}, scraping the bottom.
Boil until reduced by about two-thirds.

Lower to medium and add the tomatoes. Blend briefly with a #stick blender{},
~{15-20%seconds}, to a rough, even sauce.

Return the meat and add the @carrot{1}(peeled, whole), @bay leaves{2} and a
pinch of salt. Bring to a simmer.

Cover and bake ~{1%hour}. Lower the oven to 225°F for ~{1%hour}, then to
200°F. It is done at about ~{3%hours} in all, when a knife slides easily into
the oxtail and the carrot.

> The oxtail lets go some fat; skim it each hour if you like.

> A Montepulciano d'Abruzzo or Nero d'Avola suits the wine.
